  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nStalled front in the central and east will push eastward through\nthis evening as will a secondary front and upper level west \nmoving in from Montana. A few showers and perhaps isolated\nstrong to severe thunderstorms are still possible along the\nstalled front through this evening. The stronger storms should\nmainly be confined to the James River Valley and eastward as a\ngradient of instability will remain into this evening along with\nhigh amounts of shear. Quarter sized hail and winds to 60 mph\nwill be the main threats. Meanwhile, the secondary disturbance\ncould bring a band of mainly showers from west to east through\nthis evening with some isolated thunderstorms. Wildfire smoke\nwill also linger through this evening, with the secondary front\nclearing out smoke tonight. Lastly, a Red Flag Warning remains\nin place across southwestern and south central portions for\nlower RH and gusty winds into this evening. Tonight, clearing\nskies and lowering winds will bring much cooler temperatures as\na surface high moves in. Look for lows in the 40s to lower 50s\nas a result. Some patchy fog is possible, although a steady\nnorthwest wind should limit this potential. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday will overall be dry with cooler\ntemperatures an some breezy northwest winds. Highs on Tuesday\nwill be in the 70s, with slightly warmer highs in the mid 70s to\nmid 80s on Wednesday. Exiting surface high with northwest flow\naloft could bring some breezy northwest winds. Meanwhile, low RH\nvalues could be found each day especially in the southwest. If\nthese breezy winds and RH values can align then at least near\ncritical fire weather conditions are possible. Currently the\nstronger winds look to be north and east of these lower RH\nvalues, thus will have to monitor for any shifts in the\nforecast. \n\nThursday through the end of the week, upper level ridge builds\nand moves inland. Temperatures will depending on if this ridge \nflattens and brings zonal flow or stays robust. Both solutions \nbring a general warming trend to end the week. Highs in the 80s \nand 90s are forecast as a result, yet NBM spreads indicate it \ncould be warmer. Look for lows generally in the 50s to 60s \nduring this time period. This appears to be a mainly dry pattern\nto end the week, unless any weak disturbances in northwest flow\ncan bring a few showers. With this dry pattern afternoon \nhumidity values look to remain low through the end of the week. \nWinds for now look to overall be light, yet will have to also \nmonitor for any changes as gusty winds and low RH could bring at\nleast some increased fire weather concerns at times during this\ntime period.",
